  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 2:10 AM EDT...Current satellite and radar\nare mainly quiet across the region, with just a few mid and \nhigh clouds around. A few of the more sheltered areas in the \nhigher terrain have decoupled, allowing temps there to drop into\nthe mid to upper 30s. However, for the rest of the rest of the \nregion, a light winds has kept the boundary layer mixed, with \ntemperatures in the 40s to low 50s. Temperatures today will end \nup about 3-5 degrees cooler than yesterday for most areas. It \nwill be breezy again thanks to deep BL mixing and a relatively \ntight pressure gradient ahead of an approaching sfc high. A weak\nupper impulse may lead to a few isolated light showers late \nthis afternoon or early evening across the ADKs, upper Hudson \nValley, and southern VT.\n\nSfc high pressure builds overhead tonight. With clear skies and \ndiminishing winds, temperatures should radiate into the 30s to low \n40s for most of the area. Some patchy frost will be possible, \nalthough the best chance will be mainly in the higher elevations, so \nin coordination with WFO BTV we decided to hold off on any frost \nadvisories for the upper Hudson Valley. After a cold start to the \nday Saturday, temps climb back into the 50s and 60s for afternoon \nhighs. With the sfc high firmly overhead and rising heights aloft \nleading to subsidence, it should remain dry, making for a beautiful \nfall day. While high clouds increase overnight, our proximity to the \nsfc high should allow temperatures to drop quickly after sunset \nSaturday night. We continue the trend from the previous forecast of \ngoing below NBM temps with 30s (southern VT, Berkshires, upper \nHudson Valley)to 40s (rest of the region).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Sunday, remnant moisture associated with what is \ncurrently Hurricane Isaias will make its way up the east coast, with \nthe weakened sfc low tracking to the south of Long Island Sunday \nnight. PWATs rise to 1.25-1.5\", while mid-level warm advection, \nFGEN/convergence at the nose of the LLJ, and the right entrance \nregion of an upper jet streak to our north will provide forcing for \nascent. Latest guidance seems to be coming into better agreement on \nthe track, with measurable rain now expected all the way up into the \nADKs. Timing has also sped up slightly, so it now looks like rain \nwill begin Sunday afternoon and end early Monday morning. Total \nrainfall amounts generally look to be on the order of 0.25 to 0.75\" \nwith some locally higher amounts up to an inch or so possible near I-\n84. Therefore, with very dry antecedent conditions, there is no \nconcern for flooding with this system. Temperatures Sunday have also \ntrended slightly cooler (highs 50s to mid 60s) given earlier arrival \ntime of the rain. Monday has also now trended drier, with sfc high \npressure expected to build into the region by the afternoon.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Tuesday or Tuesday night, there is decent agreement \nfrom deterministic and ensemble guidance for a strong cold front to \ncross our region. The front may bring with it some additional \nchances for showers. Behind the front, a chilly airmass builds into \nthe region as does a broad area of high pressure. This will \nresult in quiet weather conditions Wednesday and Thursday, \nalthough Wednesday may be a little breezy. Temperatures behind \nthe front will average a few to several degrees below normal, \nwith both nights being noticeably chilly as lows dip into the \n30s to low 40s for many areas.",
